    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-at-sea-act-2000-sch-2-sec-6",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "6 Application of Australian criminal law outside the adjacent area",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-at-sea-act-2000#sch-2-sec-6"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-at-sea-act-2000.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-at-sea-act-2000.pdf, p.9",
      "values:originalText": "(1) The substantive criminal law of the Jervis Bay Territory, as in\nforce from time to time, applies at sea outside the adjacent area to a\ncriminal act:\n\n(a) on an Australian ship; or\n\n(b) in the course of activities controlled from an Australian ship;\nor\n\n(c) of a person who has abandoned, or temporarily left, an\nAustralian ship and has not returned to land.\n\n(2) The substantive criminal law of the Jervis Bay Territory, as in\nforce from time to time, applies at sea outside the adjacent area to a\ncriminal act:\n\n(a) of an Australian citizen (other than a member of the crew) on\na foreign ship; or\n\n(b) of an Australian citizen (other than a member of the crew) in\nthe course of activities controlled from a foreign ship; or\n\n(c) of an Australian citizen who has abandoned, or temporarily\nleft, a foreign ship and has not returned to land.\n\n(3) The substantive criminal law of the Jervis Bay Territory, as in\nforce from time to time, is taken to have applied at sea outside the\nadjacent area to a criminal act:\n\n(a) on a foreign ship; or\n\n(b) in the course of activities controlled from a foreign ship; or\n\n(c) of a person who has abandoned, or temporarily left, a foreign\nship and has not returned to land;\nif the first country at which the ship calls, or the person lands, after\nthe criminal act, is Australia or an external territory of Australia.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) The substantive criminal law of the Jervis Bay Territory, as in\nforce from time to time, applies at sea outside the adjacent area to a\ncriminal act:\n\n(a) on an Australian ship; or\n\n(b) in the course of activities controlled from an Australian ship;\nor\n\n(c) of a person who has abandoned, or temporarily left, an\nAustralian ship and has not returned to land.\n\n(2) The substantive criminal law of the Jervis Bay Territory, as in\nforce from time to time, applies at sea outside the adjacent area to a\ncriminal act:\n\n(a) of an Australian citizen (other than a member of the crew) on\na foreign ship; or\n\n(b) of an Australian citizen (other than a member of the crew) in\nthe course of activities controlled from a foreign ship; or\n\n(c) of an Australian citizen who has abandoned, or temporarily\nleft, a foreign ship and has not returned to land.\n\n(3) The substantive criminal law of the Jervis Bay Territory, as in\nforce from time to time, is taken to have applied at sea outside the\nadjacent area to a criminal act:\n\n(a) on a foreign ship; or\n\n(b) in the course of activities controlled from a foreign ship; or\n\n(c) of a person who has abandoned, or temporarily left, a foreign\nship and has not returned to land;\nif the first country at which the ship calls, or the person lands, after\nthe criminal act, is Australia or an external territory of Australia.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-at-sea-act-2000-sch-2-sec-6-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-at-sea-act-2000-sch-2-sec-6-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-at-sea-act-2000-sch-2-sec-6-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "cbcebce4db39f91412572cc41e7943eb093425c498f6375231b7ec7177dde429"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-at-sea-act-2000#sch-2-sec-6",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "6 Application of Australian criminal law outside the adjacent area",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 9,
      "values:originalText": "(1) The substantive criminal law of the Jervis Bay Territory, as in\nforce from time to time, applies at sea outside the adjacent area to a\ncriminal act:\n\n(a) on an Australian ship; or\n\n(b) in the course of activities controlled from an Australian ship;\nor\n\n(c) of a person who has abandoned, or temporarily left, an\nAustralian ship and has not returned to land.\n\n(2) The substantive criminal law of the Jervis Bay Territory, as in\nforce from time to time, applies at sea outside the adjacent area to a\ncriminal act:\n\n(a) of an Australian citizen (other than a member of the crew) on\na foreign ship; or\n\n(b) of an Australian citizen (other than a member of the crew) in\nthe course of activities controlled from a foreign ship; or\n\n(c) of an Australian citizen who has abandoned, or temporarily\nleft, a foreign ship and has not returned to land.\n\n(3) The substantive criminal law of the Jervis Bay Territory, as in\nforce from time to time, is taken to have applied at sea outside the\nadjacent area to a criminal act:\n\n(a) on a foreign ship; or\n\n(b) in the course of activities controlled from a foreign ship; or\n\n(c) of a person who has abandoned, or temporarily left, a foreign\nship and has not returned to land;\nif the first country at which the ship calls, or the person lands, after\nthe criminal act, is Australia or an external territory of Australia."
    },
