    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-at-sea-act-2000-sch-1-sec-2",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "2 Application of State criminal law in adjacent area",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-at-sea-act-2000#sch-1-sec-2"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-at-sea-act-2000.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-at-sea-act-2000.pdf, p.21",
      "values:originalText": "(1) The substantive criminal law of a State, as in force from time to\ntime, applies, by force of the law of the State, throughout the inner\nadjacent area for the State.\n\n(2) The provisions of the substantive criminal law of a State, as in\nforce from time to time, apply, by force of the law of the\nCommonwealth, throughout the outer adjacent area for the State.\n\n(3) However, this clause does not:\n\n(a) apply to a substantive criminal law that is incapable of\napplying in an adjacent area or is limited by its express terms\nto a place within the area of a State; or\nExample:\nA law making it an offence to drive a motor vehicle at a\nspeed exceeding a prescribed limit on a road could not apply in\nan adjacent area because of the inherent localising elements of\nthe offence. The scheme does not therefore purport to extend the\napplication of such a law to the adjacent area.\n\n(b) give a legal effect to a provision of a substantive criminal law\nthat the provision does not have within the area of the State.\nExample:\nIf the effect of a provision of the substantive criminal law\nof a State is limited under section 109 of the Constitution within\nthe area of the State, the effect is similarly limited in the outer\nadjacent area for the State even though the provision applies in\nthe outer adjacent area under the legislative authority of the\nCommonwealth.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) The substantive criminal law of a State, as in force from time to\ntime, applies, by force of the law of the State, throughout the inner\nadjacent area for the State.\n\n(2) The provisions of the substantive criminal law of a State, as in\nforce from time to time, apply, by force of the law of the\nCommonwealth, throughout the outer adjacent area for the State.\n\n(3) However, this clause does not:\n\n(a) apply to a substantive criminal law that is incapable of\napplying in an adjacent area or is limited by its express terms\nto a place within the area of a State; or\nExample:\nA law making it an offence to drive a motor vehicle at a\nspeed exceeding a prescribed limit on a road could not apply in\nan adjacent area because of the inherent localising elements of\nthe offence. The scheme does not therefore purport to extend the\napplication of such a law to the adjacent area.\n\n(b) give a legal effect to a provision of a substantive criminal law\nthat the provision does not have within the area of the State.\nExample:\nIf the effect of a provision of the substantive criminal law\nof a State is limited under section 109 of the Constitution within\nthe area of the State, the effect is similarly limited in the outer\nadjacent area for the State even though the provision applies in\nthe outer adjacent area under the legislative authority of the\nCommonwealth.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-at-sea-act-2000-sch-1-sec-2-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-at-sea-act-2000-sch-1-sec-2-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-at-sea-act-2000-sch-1-sec-2-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "3e80647cea530162f33193cbcacaabe89d9aa9252b1324f90c780d239961e773"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-at-sea-act-2000#sch-1-sec-2",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "2 Application of State criminal law in adjacent area",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 21,
      "values:originalText": "(1) The substantive criminal law of a State, as in force from time to\ntime, applies, by force of the law of the State, throughout the inner\nadjacent area for the State.\n\n(2) The provisions of the substantive criminal law of a State, as in\nforce from time to time, apply, by force of the law of the\nCommonwealth, throughout the outer adjacent area for the State.\n\n(3) However, this clause does not:\n\n(a) apply to a substantive criminal law that is incapable of\napplying in an adjacent area or is limited by its express terms\nto a place within the area of a State; or\nExample:\nA law making it an offence to drive a motor vehicle at a\nspeed exceeding a prescribed limit on a road could not apply in\nan adjacent area because of the inherent localising elements of\nthe offence. The scheme does not therefore purport to extend the\napplication of such a law to the adjacent area.\n\n(b) give a legal effect to a provision of a substantive criminal law\nthat the provision does not have within the area of the State.\nExample:\nIf the effect of a provision of the substantive criminal law\nof a State is limited under section 109 of the Constitution within\nthe area of the State, the effect is similarly limited in the outer\nadjacent area for the State even though the provision applies in\nthe outer adjacent area under the legislative authority of the\nCommonwealth."
    },
